[[:db/retract 17592186188550 :question/answer-fields 17592186185580] {:db/id 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" "question-e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" "question-4893e2e1-c5c0-525e-90bd-45f0ee89d95b" "question-062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" "question-b59e493b-eefe-51a6-8a6a-b1643795634d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188215 :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$ \n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f3ea39b0-84ca-56ad-ab8f-d5ad95ed12c9" "question-dea03ebf-107d-593b-bf22-bd12e437ac5e" "question-1599a91b-303e-58a8-b434-5ef4c1a036ef" "question-264130f3-38ba-510d-80d5-15a003677fae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188214 :question/problem "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} & =(12.5)^{2}+(11.7)^{2}+(13.2)^{2}+(12.8)^{2}+(12.1)^{2} \\\\  & =156.25+136.89+174.24+163.84+146.41 \\\\  & =777.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" :question/id #uuid "5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" :question/math-academy-id "q-139813" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu })}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions I and II are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d48c9bdb-925a-55e9-8810-2371ff4500d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d48c9bdb-925a-55e9-8810-2371ff4500d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d48c9bdb-925a-55e9-8810-2371ff4500d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" "answer-e5fa121b-5ca3-59a5-9ca2-3cfda696c7f3" "answer-312870f5-b5f4-5f45-a06d-0ca470866f05" "answer-ea421d4e-7720-57f3-94e7-9b66a798936e" "answer-363a8247-f9e4-5fde-b5a0-221b67b24dbd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer/id #uuid "24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5fa121b-5ca3-59a5-9ca2-3cfda696c7f3" :answer/id #uuid "e5fa121b-5ca3-59a5-9ca2-3cfda696c7f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-312870f5-b5f4-5f45-a06d-0ca470866f05" :answer/id #uuid "312870f5-b5f4-5f45-a06d-0ca470866f05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea421d4e-7720-57f3-94e7-9b66a798936e" :answer/id #uuid "ea421d4e-7720-57f3-94e7-9b66a798936e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-363a8247-f9e4-5fde-b5a0-221b67b24dbd" :answer/id #uuid "363a8247-f9e4-5fde-b5a0-221b67b24dbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/id #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/math-academy-id "q-139814" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" "answer-be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" "answer-e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" "answer-c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" "answer-05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" :answer/id #uuid "2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" :answer/id #uuid "e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" :answer/id #uuid "c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" :answer/id #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4893e2e1-c5c0-525e-90bd-45f0ee89d95b" :question/id #uuid "4893e2e1-c5c0-525e-90bd-45f0ee89d95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143552" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\mu \\cdot \\sigma -\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\mu -\\text{median}({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\sigma -max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{15})$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expressions I and II are *not* statistics. They are functions that contain the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression III is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3688953c-5564-5430-b587-22b1cb1cae35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3688953c-5564-5430-b587-22b1cb1cae35" :answer-field/id #uuid "3688953c-5564-5430-b587-22b1cb1cae35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba0857c3-5c1b-52e2-a099-8d64c550ebdc" "answer-f2f31955-0b4d-58d4-8a01-1883943c46ab" "answer-daab6621-7a59-5df9-94ae-46892b25c2d3" "answer-85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" "answer-80e7de96-4120-5130-bf63-a0d6c2259820"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba0857c3-5c1b-52e2-a099-8d64c550ebdc" :answer/id #uuid "ba0857c3-5c1b-52e2-a099-8d64c550ebdc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2f31955-0b4d-58d4-8a01-1883943c46ab" :answer/id #uuid "f2f31955-0b4d-58d4-8a01-1883943c46ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daab6621-7a59-5df9-94ae-46892b25c2d3" :answer/id #uuid "daab6621-7a59-5df9-94ae-46892b25c2d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer/id #uuid "85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80e7de96-4120-5130-bf63-a0d6c2259820" :answer/id #uuid "80e7de96-4120-5130-bf63-a0d6c2259820" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" :question/id #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139820" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n- $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" "answer-7e200172-34ca-5ab5-a0aa-e59cf6e4d94a" "answer-8162f8